WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.787
- We are talking about
the view matrix today.

00:00:02.787 --> 00:00:05.295
What are we viewing?
Why is it called view matrix?

00:00:05.295 --> 00:00:07.840
- It's the view from
the camera's point of view.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.600
- Ah, so view camera.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.400
Okay, and it's a matrix,
which means it takes us

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.065
from one space to another.

00:00:16.065 --> 00:00:19.568
This particular matrix
takes us from world space

00:00:19.568 --> 00:00:21.790
to camera space.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:23.472
Now, this is where
Scott and I

00:00:23.472 --> 00:00:25.815
argue a lot about...

00:00:25.815 --> 00:00:27.334
- Go ahead.
Bring it on.

00:00:27.334 --> 00:00:28.834
You bring it on,
and I'll correct you.

00:00:28.834 --> 00:00:30.391
- Thank you.

00:00:30.391 --> 00:00:32.200
Because we argue
about the camera a lot.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.595
Like, I tend to do all my work

00:00:34.595 --> 00:00:37.240
as a physics programmer
in world space.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.207
I like moving the camera around.

00:00:39.207 --> 00:00:41.648
I shoot rays out of the camera.

00:00:41.648 --> 00:00:45.192
So it has a position
and an orientation,

00:00:45.192 --> 00:00:48.045
but Scott says no.
- It doesn't.

00:00:48.045 --> 00:00:49.577
And I do the math
 to help you

00:00:49.577 --> 00:00:51.602
do all that stuff,
so let me explain.

00:00:51.602 --> 00:00:54.152
The camera is at
the origin looking down

00:00:54.152 --> 00:00:56.565
the negative Z axis,
 and rather than moving

00:00:56.565 --> 00:00:58.120
the camera around, you move

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.700
the universe around.
- Yeah.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.100
- That's just how
it works, mathematically.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.620
That is how it works.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.734
Now, I know a lot
of people need...

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:08.673
they need the camera
to move because it's...

00:01:08.673 --> 00:01:10.636
My way of looking at it is not

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:12.302
the intuitive way
of looking at it.

00:01:12.302 --> 00:01:13.893
That'll throw people off.

00:01:13.893 --> 00:01:16.342
So I have to fake all this stuff

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:18.228
by saying, yeah,
the camera can move,

00:01:18.228 --> 00:01:21.240
but really, I'm moving
the universe around

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.755
the non-moving camera,

00:01:22.755 --> 00:01:24.285
but I just need to,

00:01:24.285 --> 00:01:26.362
you know, do some
secret things in the math

00:01:26.362 --> 00:01:27.875
that I'm not going
to tell you about.

00:01:27.875 --> 00:01:29.763
And then you believe
that the camera...

00:01:29.763 --> 00:01:31.894
Oh, look at me.
The camera's moving.

00:01:31.894 --> 00:01:33.412
- Yeah, true.
I end up believing

00:01:33.412 --> 00:01:35.300
the camera exists,
but it's all just mathematics.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.140
So we have different
views on this.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.875
Nice, I'm glad you
smiled at that.

00:01:41.875 --> 00:01:43.996
In fact, the way I code this
is if we take

00:01:43.996 --> 00:01:46.610
the projection matrix,
which we talked about,

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.617
and you take the view matrix,
we multiply those two together,

00:01:49.617 --> 00:01:51.385
that does make the camera.

00:01:51.385 --> 00:01:54.249
And so you can view it as

00:01:54.249 --> 00:01:56.550
the view matrix
is the orientation,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.508
position, location
of the camera,

00:01:58.508 --> 00:02:00.402
and then the
projection matrix is

00:02:00.402 --> 00:02:02.980
the lens on the camera.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:04.676
- Oh, I like that.
Yeah.

00:02:04.676 --> 00:02:07.074
- So I combine those,
 and now you've got

00:02:07.074 --> 00:02:09.007
the whole camera set up,
and the model's just the actor

00:02:09.007 --> 00:02:11.220
running around on stage
doing something.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.625
- Yeah, just like Cassidy has.

00:02:13.625 --> 00:02:15.225
All the models were set up.

00:02:15.225 --> 00:02:17.502
Now, we have a video
on world space where,

00:02:17.502 --> 00:02:19.204
in that video, you see

00:02:19.204 --> 00:02:21.965
Cassidy's actually moving
the camera around,

00:02:21.965 --> 00:02:23.945
and we've got the counter
and everything.

00:02:23.945 --> 00:02:26.520
So maybe we can start
in world space.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:28.253
And then we'll get to

00:02:28.253 --> 00:02:29.913
camera space in a bit.

00:02:29.913 --> 00:02:32.380
Oh, and this is the origin.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:33.880
I should have introduced this.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.595
These are the axes.

00:02:35.595 --> 00:02:37.870
So Y goes up, X across,

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.815
Z towards you.

00:02:40.815 --> 00:02:43.669
So Scott is adamant
 that the camera is

00:02:43.669 --> 00:02:46.130
at the origin looking down

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.711
negative Z.

00:02:47.711 --> 00:02:49.444
Now, I want to
look at this cup.

00:02:49.444 --> 00:02:51.093
Imagine the whole...

00:02:51.093 --> 00:02:53.182
All our models,
it's just one cup.

00:02:53.182 --> 00:02:54.735
It has vertices.

00:02:54.735 --> 00:02:56.580
It has normals.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.191
I want the camera
to look at that.

00:02:58.191 --> 00:03:00.420
And I'm in world space.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.556
So what I would do
is I would

00:03:02.556 --> 00:03:04.576
rotate the camera...

00:03:06.351 --> 00:03:08.185
over here, and say
I wanted to zoom in.

00:03:08.185 --> 00:03:09.810
I would

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.565
translate

00:03:11.565 --> 00:03:13.105
to the object.

00:03:13.105 --> 00:03:15.785
And it's almost like
the camera has its own

00:03:15.785 --> 00:03:18.545
model matrix,
in a weird way.

00:03:18.545 --> 00:03:20.347
Because a model matrix,

00:03:20.347 --> 00:03:22.115
if I do the motion again,

00:03:22.115 --> 00:03:24.208
a model matrix is

00:03:24.208 --> 00:03:26.548
a rotate

00:03:26.548 --> 00:03:28.560
and a translate,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.247
if I'm skipping the scale.

00:03:30.247 --> 00:03:32.437
We don't need to
scale the camera for this.

00:03:32.437 --> 00:03:34.231
- Now, the correct way
to think about it...

00:03:34.231 --> 00:03:35.875
- Yeah, of course.

00:03:35.875 --> 00:03:38.755
- Is to put the camera
back at the origin,

00:03:38.755 --> 00:03:40.275
where you had it.

00:03:40.275 --> 00:03:41.867
And now you want to
see this cup.

00:03:41.867 --> 00:03:43.390
- Yes.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:44.982
- So move the cup
in front of the camera.

00:03:44.982 --> 00:03:46.515
- I agree.

00:03:46.515 --> 00:03:48.515
- And you want to look closer?
- Yes.

00:03:48.515 --> 00:03:50.029
- At the at the cup?
- Yes.

00:03:50.029 --> 00:03:52.126
- Move it closer.
- Oh.

00:03:52.126 --> 00:03:54.665
And that's the same thing
as moving the camera forward.

00:03:54.665 --> 00:03:57.115
- Yeah, except mathematically,

00:03:57.115 --> 00:03:59.330
this is the way
we're doing it.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:01.635
So you do it any way you want.
I'll do it right.

00:04:01.635 --> 00:04:03.186
- And we are going to agree.
I'm sure...

00:04:03.186 --> 00:04:04.734
- Ultimately, we agree.

00:04:04.734 --> 00:04:06.267
- Yes, it's weird.

00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:08.023
The arguments we have,
we usually

00:04:08.023 --> 00:04:10.618
are talking about the same
thing, but in different ways.

00:04:10.618 --> 00:04:12.288
- Well, if you and I were
just out in

00:04:12.288 --> 00:04:15.085
the middle of space someplace
with no other reference point,

00:04:15.085 --> 00:04:17.920
if you move towards me or I move
towards you, who's moving?

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.644
Who cares?

00:04:19.644 --> 00:04:21.736
- So the camera's at the origin,

00:04:21.736 --> 00:04:24.801
looking down negative Z.
Okay.

00:04:24.801 --> 00:04:27.563
Now I'm going to
rotate this camera,

00:04:27.563 --> 00:04:29.117
because I want
to look at this cup,

00:04:29.117 --> 00:04:32.445
and then I'm going to
translate it.

00:04:32.445 --> 00:04:33.965
Okay, so this is world space.

00:04:33.965 --> 00:04:36.685
I've got a nice view
of this cup.

00:04:36.685 --> 00:04:38.365
Now, Scott's certainly right.

00:04:38.365 --> 00:04:41.387
For the next stage,
 camera space,

00:04:41.387 --> 00:04:44.285
this camera
has to be at the origin.

00:04:44.285 --> 00:04:47.450
It has to be looking
down negative Z.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:49.770
So there's two
moves we could do.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.010
This camera has
a translation, right?

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:54.019
What we could do is

00:04:54.019 --> 00:04:57.297
invert the translation.

00:04:57.297 --> 00:04:58.951
Say the camera moved

00:04:58.951 --> 00:05:01.413
one way, to your left,

00:05:01.413 --> 00:05:02.939
move it back.

00:05:02.939 --> 00:05:05.205
But the thing is you're not
moving the camera at this point.

00:05:05.205 --> 00:05:06.701
It's all just conceptual.

00:05:06.701 --> 00:05:08.475
You're moving all the vertices

00:05:08.475 --> 00:05:10.358
and normals

00:05:10.358 --> 00:05:11.917
on that cup.

00:05:11.917 --> 00:05:14.130
Well, not the normals.
I'll get to that in a second.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.615
So the first move
 to get a camera space

00:05:16.615 --> 00:05:20.050
is called an inverse
translation.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:21.713
Notice the camera is still
looking at the cup.

00:05:21.713 --> 00:05:23.222
The second move...

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:24.715
Scott's still not
happy about this.

00:05:24.715 --> 00:05:26.822
The camera's at
the origin right now,

00:05:26.822 --> 00:05:28.875
but it's not looking
down negative Z.

00:05:28.875 --> 00:05:31.595
So now we've got to
undo the rotation.

00:05:31.595 --> 00:05:33.535
So the next move is called

00:05:33.535 --> 00:05:36.355
the inverse rotation,

00:05:36.355 --> 00:05:38.623
and we're applying it

00:05:38.623 --> 00:05:41.810
to the cup's vertices
and the normals, in this case.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:45.109
If the vertices have
a W of one,

00:05:45.109 --> 00:05:46.981
they can translate.

00:05:46.981 --> 00:05:48.946
But you don't want
your normals translating.

00:05:48.946 --> 00:05:51.124
If you move to the right,
you don't want your normal

00:05:51.124 --> 00:05:53.302
to point more to the right,

00:05:53.302 --> 00:05:55.815
but they do rotate.

00:05:55.815 --> 00:05:57.575
So those are the two moves.

00:05:57.575 --> 00:06:01.015
And because these are matrices,
you just multiply them together.

00:06:01.015 --> 00:06:02.535
Multiply first the input...

00:06:02.535 --> 00:06:04.535
- Put this back the way it was.
- Of course, of course.

00:06:04.535 --> 00:06:06.135
- Put it back the way it was.

00:06:06.135 --> 00:06:07.950
You had it like this,

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.250
then had it translate...
- Yes, yes.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:11.755
That's what I have.
- All right.

00:06:11.755 --> 00:06:14.010
- Because this camera
has a location and orientation.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:15.730
Sorry, go ahead.
- I grant you.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:17.960
Why do you need to take
 the inverse

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.291
instead of just,
if you know you translated it

00:06:20.291 --> 00:06:23.045
a few units this way,
just go back the other way.

00:06:23.045 --> 00:06:26.405
Just go the other direction
rather than the inverse.

00:06:26.405 --> 00:06:29.021
Granted that the inverse
 of the translation

00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:31.388
is just going the other way,
why are you bothering

00:06:31.388 --> 00:06:34.317
with the full inverse?
- Yeah, good point.

00:06:34.317 --> 00:06:36.547
- Just go the other way.
- Yeah, I guess the inverse

00:06:36.547 --> 00:06:38.180
of the translation is going
back the other way.

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:39.704
- Yeah, and the same thing
with your rotate.

00:06:39.704 --> 00:06:42.833
So if you rotated 90 degrees,
rotate negative 90 degrees.

00:06:42.833 --> 00:06:44.478
- Yes.

00:06:44.478 --> 00:06:46.311
I guess the reason I'm saying

00:06:46.311 --> 00:06:48.825
invert the translation matrix

00:06:48.825 --> 00:06:51.063
is that a lot
 of people like to have

00:06:51.063 --> 00:06:53.615
a model matrix for the camera.

00:06:53.615 --> 00:06:56.415
Maybe not yourself,
but some people do.

00:06:56.415 --> 00:06:58.207
They have a model matrix
 for the camera,

00:06:58.207 --> 00:07:00.728
and the model matrix
does two things.

00:07:00.728 --> 00:07:03.002
Rotates,

00:07:03.002 --> 00:07:04.526
then translates.

00:07:04.526 --> 00:07:07.533
And what they like to do,
these fancy people,

00:07:07.533 --> 00:07:09.336
is that they have

00:07:09.336 --> 00:07:11.685
a translation matrix
on the left,

00:07:11.685 --> 00:07:13.400
a rotation matrix on the right.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.025
They're multiplied together.
Order matters.

00:07:16.025 --> 00:07:18.257
When you inverse
this whole matrix,

00:07:18.257 --> 00:07:20.585
it actually swaps the order.

00:07:20.585 --> 00:07:23.007
It actually does
an inverse translation

00:07:23.007 --> 00:07:26.185
and then an inverse rotation.

00:07:26.185 --> 00:07:28.523
So the reason I'm saying inverse
so much is just

00:07:28.523 --> 00:07:31.195
to split it into two parts.

00:07:31.195 --> 00:07:32.860
But you are correct.

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:35.493
You don't need to inverse
the matrix for a translation.

00:07:35.493 --> 00:07:37.175
Just go the other way.

00:07:37.175 --> 00:07:38.725
Now, if you did it my way,
you wouldn't have to do

00:07:38.725 --> 00:07:41.035
any of this stuff.
- How would you do it?

00:07:41.035 --> 00:07:43.300
- Leave the camera at the origin

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:45.915
looking down
the negative Z axis.

00:07:45.915 --> 00:07:48.321
And then, if you want to

00:07:48.321 --> 00:07:51.595
move the cup, translate it.

00:07:51.595 --> 00:07:53.806
And then if you want
to move it from here,

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:55.942
say to over here
and you want to rotate it,

00:07:55.942 --> 00:07:57.654
just rotate.

00:07:57.654 --> 00:08:00.245
Now the only problem is,
Cassidy's not going to like it

00:08:00.245 --> 00:08:03.542
because she has to
rotate the opposite...

00:08:03.542 --> 00:08:05.240
- Yes.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:08.200
- Opposite direction,
or the opposite axis.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:10.183
So if you're going
to rotate here around...

00:08:10.183 --> 00:08:12.727
This is the Y axis.
- Yes.

00:08:12.727 --> 00:08:14.695
So she would rotate
 from here to, say,

00:08:14.695 --> 00:08:16.885
rotate to that location.

00:08:16.885 --> 00:08:19.614
She could rotate
positive number of degrees,

00:08:19.614 --> 00:08:21.525
so that would make sense to her,
but she'd have to rotate

00:08:21.525 --> 00:08:24.260
around the negative of the axis.

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:26.254
And then that would put
it in the right place,

00:08:26.254 --> 00:08:28.831
or just put a minus sign
on the number of degrees.

00:08:28.831 --> 00:08:30.340
- Yeah, isn't it amazing?

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:31.886
- I'm going to do that
in a minute for you

00:08:31.886 --> 00:08:34.011
when we look at LookAt.
- LookAt, yes.

00:08:34.011 --> 00:08:36.020
We'll get to
another way to do this.

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:38.055
But to answer your question,

00:08:38.055 --> 00:08:40.215
why am I making it
so complicated?

00:08:40.215 --> 00:08:42.594
It's because I teach physics,

00:08:42.594 --> 00:08:45.495
and I have objects
in world space.

00:08:45.495 --> 00:08:48.215
So this object
has an X, Y, Z.

00:08:48.215 --> 00:08:50.135
W is one for its vertices.

00:08:50.135 --> 00:08:52.730
Anyways, I'm shooting
rays out of the camera.

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:55.210
I'm trying to click
on things in my course.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:58.010
So it's really handy to know
 where is the camera

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:00.250
in world space.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:01.767
Eventually, we agree.

00:09:01.767 --> 00:09:04.935
When we get to camera space,
that's the next space.

00:09:04.935 --> 00:09:07.328
The camera is necessarily
 at the origin

00:09:07.328 --> 00:09:09.470
looking down negative Z,
and that's what I do

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:11.735
for my snooker assignment.

00:09:11.735 --> 00:09:14.057
For my snooker assignment,
I want to whack the ball

00:09:14.057 --> 00:09:16.354
the direction that
the camera's looking,

00:09:16.354 --> 00:09:19.093
and that's always negative Z
in your space.

00:09:19.093 --> 00:09:21.217
Yeah, maybe if I could just
repeat that one more time

00:09:21.217 --> 00:09:23.980
because it's really subtle,
what we're doing.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:26.359
So imagine the camera

00:09:26.359 --> 00:09:29.508
is at the origin, looking
down negative Z.

00:09:29.508 --> 00:09:31.015
Everyone's happy.

00:09:31.015 --> 00:09:32.947
Now, Scott's not going
to be happy because I'm going to

00:09:32.947 --> 00:09:35.034
rotate the camera,

00:09:35.034 --> 00:09:37.748
and then translate it, because
I really want to look at...

00:09:37.748 --> 00:09:39.262
Oh, it's a bit off angle now.

00:09:39.262 --> 00:09:42.055
I really want
to look at this cup.

00:09:42.055 --> 00:09:44.426
Now, this view matrix takes us

00:09:44.426 --> 00:09:46.894
from world space
to camera space,

00:09:46.894 --> 00:09:49.158
and the only difference
 between those two

00:09:49.158 --> 00:09:51.326
is your frame of reference.

00:09:51.326 --> 00:09:53.131
That's one way of saying it.

00:09:53.131 --> 00:09:54.662
Nothing changes,

00:09:54.662 --> 00:09:56.479
but this camera
has to be at the origin.

00:09:56.479 --> 00:09:58.642
I can't just
move the camera back

00:09:58.642 --> 00:10:01.755
because then I'm not
as close to my object.

00:10:01.755 --> 00:10:03.275
I can't just rotate the camera.

00:10:03.275 --> 00:10:05.035
I'm not looking at the object.

00:10:05.035 --> 00:10:07.315
All these operations
are actually happening

00:10:07.315 --> 00:10:10.075
to the vertices and the normals.

00:10:10.075 --> 00:10:12.007
So the first thing
I would do is

00:10:12.007 --> 00:10:14.183
translate back,

00:10:14.183 --> 00:10:16.064
still looking at the cup,

00:10:16.064 --> 00:10:18.930
and then rotate the other way,

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:22.761
still looking at the cup,
and now we are in camera space.

00:10:22.761 --> 00:10:24.889
And because these are matrices,

00:10:24.889 --> 00:10:26.555
you just multiply
them together.

00:10:26.555 --> 00:10:28.475
And there you go.

00:10:28.475 --> 00:10:30.608
But there is another way
 to do this,

00:10:30.608 --> 00:10:32.516
and maybe you can explain this,

00:10:32.516 --> 00:10:34.955
the LookAt function.

00:10:34.955 --> 00:10:37.355
And is this an...
Is this in OpenGL?

00:10:37.355 --> 00:10:40.036
- Yeah, this is
an OpenGL function.

00:10:40.036 --> 00:10:42.528
In my class,
I tell everyone that

00:10:42.528 --> 00:10:44.820
LookAt is a lie.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.053
It lies to you.

00:10:47.053 --> 00:10:49.457
And it says that there are

00:10:49.457 --> 00:10:51.165
three things you need to know.

00:10:51.165 --> 00:10:54.925
You need to know,
where is the camera located?

00:10:54.925 --> 00:10:57.299
So the position of the camera,
 that would be

00:10:57.299 --> 00:10:59.245
what you're doing here.

00:10:59.245 --> 00:11:02.539
Then you would need
to know what is it looking at.

00:11:02.539 --> 00:11:04.831
So you're looking at this,

00:11:04.831 --> 00:11:06.330
at this cup.

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:08.810
- So that would give
it its orientation, I guess.

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:10.917
Because if I'm looking
at the cup, but I'm...

00:11:10.917 --> 00:11:12.729
- You're still look...

00:11:12.729 --> 00:11:14.648
You pick a point
out here in space,

00:11:14.648 --> 00:11:16.483
and that's what
you're looking at.

00:11:16.483 --> 00:11:18.486
So if you did move
 this thing up,

00:11:18.486 --> 00:11:21.834
it would still be
looking at that, right?

00:11:21.834 --> 00:11:24.865
And then the last one is,
which way is up?

00:11:24.865 --> 00:11:27.745
Because the camera could
be tilted over to the side.

00:11:27.745 --> 00:11:30.225
And we really haven't
talked about that one yet.

00:11:30.225 --> 00:11:31.750
- Yeah, you're right.

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:34.447
- So LookAt has
some funny features.

00:11:34.447 --> 00:11:37.286
Now, a funny thing happens
if you print out the values.

00:11:37.286 --> 00:11:39.835
It creates a view matrix,

00:11:39.835 --> 00:11:41.598
but as I mentioned earlier,

00:11:41.598 --> 00:11:43.694
everything is rotated
the other way,

00:11:43.694 --> 00:11:45.990
and the translation
goes the other way.

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:48.851
So really, what the LookAt does
is it says,

00:11:48.851 --> 00:11:51.030
let's pretend that
the camera has a location.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:53.286
Let's pretend you're
looking at that,

00:11:53.286 --> 00:11:55.203
and let's pretend this way's up,

00:11:55.203 --> 00:11:57.363
and you do all that,
and what you end up with

00:11:57.363 --> 00:11:59.952
is a rotation and a translation
 of the universe to put it

00:11:59.952 --> 00:12:01.465
in front of the camera,

00:12:01.465 --> 00:12:03.130
which is sitting at the origin,

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:04.765
looking down
the negative Z axis.

00:12:04.765 --> 00:12:06.289
- Oh, yeah.
So I should do that.

00:12:06.289 --> 00:12:09.205
So at the origin,
looking down negative Z.

00:12:09.205 --> 00:12:11.149
So we get to the same place.
- Yes, but I feel bad

00:12:11.149 --> 00:12:12.719
because I have to lie
to everybody and say,

00:12:12.719 --> 00:12:14.690
Hey, here's a 
really great call.

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:16.277
And some people,

00:12:16.277 --> 00:12:18.199
like our Paul,

00:12:18.199 --> 00:12:20.225
doesn't like the idea
that the universe moves.

00:12:20.225 --> 00:12:21.730
He wants to move the camera.

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:23.425
- Yeah, it seems more efficient
that way, isn't it?

00:12:23.425 --> 00:12:25.607
- No, not to me.

00:12:25.607 --> 00:12:27.812
And that's why we're doing
this whole series is because

00:12:27.812 --> 00:12:30.362
you have to go from space 
to space to space.

00:12:30.362 --> 00:12:33.155
And, in my view

00:12:33.155 --> 00:12:35.512
of the way all this works,

00:12:35.512 --> 00:12:38.490
it's just all there.
It's just nice and compact.

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:40.730
- Yes.
- You do it in three moves.

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:42.631
Multiply the model matrix,
the view matrix,

00:12:42.631 --> 00:12:44.183
the projection matrix,
and you're home.

00:12:44.183 --> 00:12:45.687
- Yeah, you're right.

00:12:45.687 --> 00:12:47.372
I always think
of you as the GPU.

00:12:47.372 --> 00:12:48.897
You're heading
towards the screen.

00:12:48.897 --> 00:12:51.918
You want pixels there.
- Yeah.

00:12:51.918 --> 00:12:54.456
- I wonder if it would make
more sense if the view matrix

00:12:54.456 --> 00:12:57.065
was the other way around.
Like, I don't know.

00:12:57.065 --> 00:13:00.749
I'm just thinking right now
that if the view matrix...

00:13:00.749 --> 00:13:04.030
And it doesn't, by the way,
So I might be confusing people.

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:06.062
But if the view matrix
took you from

00:13:06.062 --> 00:13:08.230
camera space to world,

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:09.994
then I guess you would translate

00:13:09.994 --> 00:13:12.671
and rotate the right way.

00:13:12.671 --> 00:13:15.443
- Yeah, now, in our classes,
when we start off

00:13:15.443 --> 00:13:18.176
looking at graphics,
we use a LookAt model

00:13:18.176 --> 00:13:20.571
and use that trick just so

00:13:20.571 --> 00:13:23.015
I can quickly get
the camera in there.

00:13:23.015 --> 00:13:24.848
But in no time,
maybe halfway through

00:13:24.848 --> 00:13:27.256
the first year
or the first semester class,

00:13:27.256 --> 00:13:29.379
we throw that all away

00:13:29.379 --> 00:13:32.031
and do it exactly the way
I just said,

00:13:32.031 --> 00:13:34.170
where we use one
of your quaternions

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:38.030
and one vector of translation,

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:40.106
and then hook those all up
and generate

00:13:40.106 --> 00:13:43.218
the view matrix manually.
- I really like that.

00:13:43.218 --> 00:13:45.245
- Two lines of code,
three lines of code.

00:13:45.245 --> 00:13:46.801
- Yeah.
- And we're done.

00:13:46.801 --> 00:13:48.714
And then it's none of this
inversion, inverting,

00:13:48.714 --> 00:13:50.525
moving.

00:13:50.525 --> 00:13:53.169
This stuff is too complicated
to make more complicated.

00:13:53.169 --> 00:13:54.748
- And, yeah,

00:13:54.748 --> 00:13:56.308
I'm not a big fan
of the LookAt

00:13:56.308 --> 00:13:58.650
because I love quaternions.

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:02.272
Like, I want to orientate
my camera with a quaternion,

00:14:02.272 --> 00:14:05.352
but the LookAt
function has an at.

00:14:05.352 --> 00:14:07.050
What are you looking at?

00:14:07.050 --> 00:14:09.310
And that determines
the orientation.

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:10.801
- That's even a lie.

00:14:10.801 --> 00:14:12.367
You don't actually have to

00:14:12.367 --> 00:14:14.199
put in the at,
you just have to put in

00:14:14.199 --> 00:14:16.378
the direction
of what you're looking at.

00:14:16.378 --> 00:14:18.688
- So it's not a position?

00:14:18.688 --> 00:14:21.447
- It's not one of my
favourite calls because

00:14:21.447 --> 00:14:23.746
if you go from here
 and you rotate over

00:14:23.746 --> 00:14:26.390
and you come around
the other side,

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:28.790
it really goes crazy.

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:31.430
- Yeah, because you've
decided which way is up.

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:34.709
So would the camera rotate?
Like, would it, like...

00:14:34.709 --> 00:14:36.710
- The whole image
just suddenly flips.

00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:38.233
- Oh, right.

00:14:38.233 --> 00:14:39.734
- Yeah, I don't like LookAt.

00:14:39.734 --> 00:14:42.135
- Yeah, but I guess maybe
it's good for, like,

00:14:42.135 --> 00:14:44.028
simple camera moves
 where you're just

00:14:44.028 --> 00:14:46.519
looking left, right, up,
down, like any kind of roll.

00:14:46.519 --> 00:14:49.115
- Yeah, so the better way
of rotating is to...

00:14:49.115 --> 00:14:51.284
As we were
talking earlier, rotating

00:14:51.284 --> 00:14:53.787
with quaternions is way better

00:14:53.787 --> 00:14:55.600
than yaw, pitch and roll.

00:14:55.600 --> 00:14:57.987
Yeah, but if you're not doing
 anything crazy,

00:14:57.987 --> 00:14:59.840
yaw, pitch and roll is
easier to comprehend.

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:01.954
- Yeah, true.
So yeah, maybe we can

00:15:01.954 --> 00:15:05.106
summarize this,
the view matrix.

00:15:05.106 --> 00:15:07.253
The view matrix has taken us

00:15:07.253 --> 00:15:09.885
from world space
to camera space.

00:15:09.885 --> 00:15:12.522
So in world space,
 my camera

00:15:12.522 --> 00:15:14.315
has a location

00:15:14.315 --> 00:15:16.010
and it's not looking
down negative Z,

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:18.334
it's looking off to your left.

00:15:18.334 --> 00:15:21.245
That's world space, but we need
to be in camera space.

00:15:21.245 --> 00:15:23.675
We need this camera
 to be at the origin

00:15:23.675 --> 00:15:26.710
looking at negative Z, but we
still want to look at the cup.

00:15:26.710 --> 00:15:28.703
So all these moves
are happening

00:15:28.703 --> 00:15:30.550
on the vertices and normals.

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:33.630
It's, like you're saying,
it's the opposite translation

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:36.729
or I call it the inverse
translation matrix.

00:15:36.729 --> 00:15:38.395
It's a bit fancy.

00:15:38.395 --> 00:15:41.450
And then it's
the opposite rotation,

00:15:41.450 --> 00:15:45.171
or you could say
the inverse rotation matrix.

00:15:45.171 --> 00:15:46.715
And you just multiply
them together.

00:15:46.715 --> 00:15:49.485
You get one matrix,
the view matrix.

00:15:49.485 --> 00:15:51.412
- Okay, so we still get along.
- We do.

00:15:51.412 --> 00:15:53.400
I'm glad to hear that.